::ENG3_U02_L03_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: Mara admired the palace windows, but her brother counted the guards at every door. What does this juxtaposition reveal? {
=The siblings view the palace differently: Mara sees beauty, while her brother sees danger.#Correct. The contrast defines their perspectives.
~Both siblings see only beauty.#The brother counts guards.
~Neither sibling notices the palace.#Both notice details.
~The palace has no guards.#The passage says guards are at every door.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The crowd cheered the new road, but Dalia looked at the garden it had replaced. What is juxtaposed? {
~Two cheering crowds#Only one crowd is mentioned.
=Public celebration and private loss#Correct. The crowd celebrates while Dalia notices what was lost.
~Two gardens#Only one garden is mentioned.
~A road and a river#No river is mentioned.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The teacher praised the perfect test scores while Sam noticed the empty desks of students who had stopped coming. What does Sam's perspective show? {
~He only cares about grades.#He notices missing students.
~He agrees the class is completely successful.#The empty desks complicate success.
=He sees a problem hidden behind the appearance of success.#Correct. The juxtaposition defines Sam's critical perspective.
~He does not notice the classroom.#He notices the desks.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The city unveiled a shining statue of justice while the courthouse steps remained cracked and crowded. Which explanation is strongest? {
~The statue proves justice is complete.#The cracked, crowded steps complicate that idea.
~The courthouse is described as perfect.#It is cracked and crowded.
~There is no contrast in the passage.#The statue and courthouse steps are contrasted.
=The juxtaposition suggests a gap between public ideals and lived reality.#Correct. The contrast reveals tension between image and reality.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: During the feast, Niko listened not to the music but to the servants whispering in the hall. What does Niko's attention reveal? {
~He ignores hidden voices.#He listens to them.
=He is aware of voices outside the celebration.#Correct. His perspective is defined by what he notices.
~He cannot hear anything.#He listens to whispering.
~He is focused only on food.#The passage says he listens to servants.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The general smiled at the parade, but the nurse watched the bandages hidden under the soldiers' sleeves. Which contrast matters most? {
~Smiling and sleeping#Sleeping is not mentioned.
~Music and silence#The bandages matter more than music here.
=Public pride and private injury#Correct. The contrast reveals the nurse's perspective.
~Rain and sunlight#Weather is not mentioned.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: A new clock tower rose over the town, but Ezra kept checking the broken watch in his pocket. What does this reveal about Ezra? {
=He is attached to personal memory while the town celebrates public progress.#Correct. The contrast defines his perspective.
~He cannot tell time at all.#He checks a watch, but meaning is deeper.
~He built the clock tower.#The passage does not say that.
~He dislikes all clocks.#He keeps checking one.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The queen called the treaty a triumph; the farmer called it another fence. What is defined through juxtaposition? {
~The weather#Weather is not included.
=Different perspectives on the same political event#Correct. The treaty means triumph to one person and restriction to another.
~A recipe#No recipe appears.
~Two identical opinions#The opinions contrast.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: As fireworks burst above the harbor, Tomas watched the dark windows of the closed shipyard. Which response is strongest? {
~Tomas is focused only on the fireworks.#He watches the shipyard.
~The shipyard is open and active.#It is closed.
=The contrast shows Tomas notices economic hardship beneath public celebration.#Correct. His perspective differs from the festive scene.
~There is no public celebration.#Fireworks show celebration.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The bride smiled for every photograph, but her sister watched her hands twist the torn ribbon. What does the sister notice? {
~Only happiness#The hands and torn ribbon suggest tension.
~The photographer's camera#The camera is not emphasized.
~The food at the wedding#Food is not mentioned.
=A sign of anxiety beneath the public smile#Correct. The juxtaposition reveals a hidden perspective.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The principal called the new uniforms a sign of unity; Luis saw how his friend's sleeves were too short. Which theme or issue does the juxtaposition suggest? {
=Uniformity can hide unequal experiences.#Correct. Luis notices inequality within the symbol of unity.
~Uniforms always solve every problem.#The passage complicates that claim.
~Luis dislikes all clothing.#The passage is about inequality, not clothing in general.
~The principal sees the sleeves too.#The passage contrasts their views.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The museum label called the sword heroic, but Ana read the names carved into the handle and grew quiet. What does Ana's perspective add? {
~She sees only decoration.#The names make her quiet.
=She sees personal cost behind a heroic object.#Correct. The contrast defines her perspective.
~She cannot read.#She reads the names.
~She wants to use the sword.#The passage does not say this.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The audience laughed at the actor's torn coat, but Mei noticed how carefully he folded it after the scene. What does Mei's perspective suggest? {
=She sees dignity where others see comedy.#Correct. She notices respectful care.
~She agrees the coat is only funny.#She notices something deeper.
~She ignores the actor.#She watches him closely.
~She leaves the theater.#This is not stated.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The captain admired the clear map; the sailor stared at the blank space beyond its edge. What is juxtaposed? {
~Two maps with the same detail#Only one map is mentioned.
=Confidence in known routes and awareness of the unknown#Correct. The contrast reveals different perspectives.
~A ship and a train#No train appears.
~Two identical sailors#The characters differ.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The festival banner said WELCOME, but Priya counted the locked doors along the street. Which explanation is strongest? {
~The town is fully welcoming.#Locked doors complicate the banner.
~Priya sees no details.#She counts doors.
=The juxtaposition shows Priya questions the town's public message.#Correct. Her perspective is shaped by the contrast.
~The banner is invisible.#It is described clearly.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The judge praised order; the child watched dust gather on the unanswered petitions. What does the child notice? {
~Only clean floors#Dust on petitions matters.
~The judge's robe#The robe is not mentioned.
~A celebration#No celebration appears.
=Neglected requests beneath official order#Correct. The contrast defines the child's perspective.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The town called the abandoned mine a monument; Rosa called it the place her father never came home from. Which answer best explains the juxtaposition? {
=It contrasts public memory with personal grief.#Correct. The mine means different things to the town and Rosa.
~It shows everyone agrees about the mine.#Rosa's view differs.
~It proves the mine is still open.#It is abandoned.
~It removes emotion from the passage.#It adds emotion.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The coach celebrated the trophy, but Andre watched the player sitting alone with ice on his knee. What does Andre's perspective emphasize? {
~Winning without cost#He notices injury.
=The cost behind victory#Correct. The juxtaposition reveals concern beneath celebration.
~The trophy's color#The color is not mentioned.
~The coach's silence#The coach celebrates.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The advertisement promised freedom, but June noticed the tiny print listing every fee. Which contrast defines June's perspective? {
=Large promise and hidden limitation#Correct. June sees the restriction beneath the promise.
~Two equal promises#The tiny print limits the promise.
~A poem and a song#Neither appears.
~A road and a river#Neither appears.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: While the guests admired the chandelier, Omar studied the cracks spreading across the ceiling above it. Which explanation is best? {
~Omar shares the guests' focus on beauty only.#He notices danger.
~The chandelier is not in the room.#It is mentioned.
~The ceiling is perfectly safe.#Cracks suggest concern.
=The contrast shows Omar sees risk beneath elegance.#Correct. His perspective differs from the guests'.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The king described the border wall as protection; the merchant called it a locked road. What does the juxtaposition reveal? {
=Different perspectives based on power and need#Correct. The king and merchant view the same wall differently.
~Both characters want the same thing.#Their descriptions contrast.
~The wall is imaginary.#The passage treats it as real.
~The merchant built the wall.#Not stated.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The textbook called the voyage discovery, but the islander's song called it arrival with iron. What does this contrast suggest? {
~Both sources describe the voyage the same way.#They contrast.
=Perspective changes how history is described.#Correct. The juxtaposition shows different views of the same event.
~Songs cannot express perspective.#The song clearly does.
~The voyage has no conflict.#The contrast suggests conflict.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The new hospital gleamed white, but Sera watched patients wait outside in the heat. Which detail defines Sera's perspective? {
~The building's whiteness only#She notices patients waiting.
~The absence of patients#Patients are present.
=The contrast between the gleaming building and people still waiting#Correct. This shows she sees an unresolved problem.
~The fact that there is no heat#The passage mentions heat.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Which statement best describes the purpose of juxtaposition in character analysis? {
~It always proves one character is wrong.#Not always; it reveals perspective.
=It places contrasting details together to reveal how a character sees a situation.#Correct. This is the key function in this lesson.
~It removes all conflict from a text.#It often creates or reveals tension.
~It means two details are exactly the same.#Juxtaposition depends on contrast.
}

::ENG3_U02_L03_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.R.03] Passage: The speech praised progress, but the narrator noticed the river behind the factory had turned gray. What does the narrator's perspective reveal? {
~Progress has no possible cost.#The gray river suggests cost.
~The river is bright and clean.#It is gray.
~The narrator ignores the setting.#The narrator notices the river.
=The narrator sees environmental damage beneath public claims of progress.#Correct. The juxtaposition defines perspective.
}
